3. PROHIBITED USES

The following are prohibited without prior written permission:

  a. Reverse engineering, decompiling or disassembling any part of this
     software, or circumventing any technical or access control.
  b. Copying, cloning or reproducing this software, its user interface, its
     database design, or its business logic, in whole or in substantial part —
     including re-implementations produced by rewriting, paraphrasing or
     translating this work into another language or framework.
  c. Operating a competing or derivative storefront, marketplace or service
     derived from this work.
  d. Automated collection: scraping, crawling beyond what robots.txt permits,
     bulk downloading, or harvesting content, listings, prices or media.
  e. Removing, obscuring or altering this notice or any copyright, attribution
     or ownership marking.

4. MACHINE LEARNING AND AUTOMATED SYSTEMS

The copyright holder does not grant permission to use this repository, any
deployed instance of it, or any content served by it, for:

  a. training, fine-tuning, pre-training, distilling or evaluating machine
     learning models, including large language models;
  b. inclusion in any dataset or corpus assembled for those purposes;
  c. retrieval-augmented generation or similar systems that reproduce
     substantial portions of this work to third parties in place of linking to
     it; or
  d. automated reconstruction of this software's design or source from its
     observable behaviour, API responses or client-side bundles.

Search engines and AI answer engines that index public pages in order to cite
and link to them, and agents fetching a single page because a person asked for
it, are not restricted by this section, provided they do not use what they
fetch for (a) or (b). An instruction from an end user does not otherwise create
a permission the copyright holder has not granted.
Operators of such systems are expected to honour this notice and the
machine-readable declarations in /robots.txt and /ai.txt.

This section states the copyright holder's position and withholding of
permission. Whether and how it binds a given party depends on applicable law.

5. PAYMENT INTEGRITY

Any attempt to obtain goods, services, credit or refunds without the
corresponding payment is prohibited and is pursued as fraud. This includes, but
is not limited to: forging, replaying or tampering with payment callbacks;
altering amounts, currencies, quantities or prices in transit; manipulating
order or refund state; exploiting race conditions in checkout, cancellation or
settlement; and submitting payment evidence for a transfer that was not made.

Prices, amounts and order state are determined by the server. A value submitted
by a client is treated as a request, never as a fact.

6. LOGGING AND EVIDENCE

Access to deployed instances is logged. Records retained include request
metadata, timestamps, source address, user agent and an audit trail of state
changes to orders, quotes, payments and payouts. These records are retained for
operational and security purposes and may be used as evidence in an
investigation or legal proceeding, and disclosed to payment providers, platform
operators or law enforcement where permitted or required.

Retention periods and the lawful basis for processing are set out in the privacy
notice for each deployment, which governs in case of conflict.

7. SECURITY RESEARCH

Good-faith vulnerability reports are welcome. Contact the owner before testing.
Do not test against production, do not access, modify or exfiltrate data
belonging to other users, do not run denial-of-service or volumetric tests, and
do not move real funds. Testing outside those limits is not authorised and
section 5 applies. No bounty is offered or implied.

